Overview
The s002 subgraph contained 22 non-ambiguous dependency and limitation candidates. The organizational-culture/leadership candidate and the culture-centred portion of the sociocultural-environment candidate were excluded. Higher-order dependencies are ranked by summed evidence_count, which indicates coding density rather than effect size. The bounded sample primarily concerns manufacturing, industrial maintenance, training and knowledge engineering; it cannot represent the wider construction sector. Digital-codification practices dominate sensing, modelling, ontology and repository applications, whereas experiential amplification dominates AR and scenario-based training.
Digitally reconfigured dependencies
1. Reliable industrial data, algorithms and computing infrastructure (61). LLM-generated maintenance-process models suffered malformed XML, latency, stochastic output and absent layout awareness. Robot-learning, handwork-recognition and scheduling applications faced hallucinations, small datasets, rigid ontologies, feature redundancy and limited transferability. Cloud availability and integration with existing systems were additional conditions. Deployment therefore requires curated industrial datasets, interoperable semantics, adequate computation, model monitoring and human validation; otherwise, codification can turn shop-floor know-how into unreliable instructions or decisions (c101;c102;c103;c119;c120;c126).
2. Task-centred industrial learning and human–machine-interface design (59). Paper manuals and two-dimensional representations imposed attention switching, cognitive load and difficulty interpreting physical assemblies. AR supplied spatial overlays and procedural feedback, but overlapping graphics, small text, static display positions, headset weight and fatigue constrained use. Knowledge tests also provided limited evidence of workplace transfer. Effective deployment is conditional on readable interfaces, staged content, error feedback, multilingual instructions, repeated practice and performance-based validation (c104;c105;c106;c107;c113).
3. Operationally robust sensing and contextual modelling (14). Handwork sensing was disrupted by visual occlusion, ambient noise and absent commercial body-conducted-sound sensors; factory heat, dust and noise reduced AR comfort. Machine modifications and failures changed operational sequences, while climatic variation altered viticulture decisions. Digital twins, wearables and predictive models consequently require environmental calibration, multimodal sensing and regular updating. Without these capabilities, captured activity may not remain valid under changing production conditions (c103;c105;c106;c108;c124).
4. Blockchain-specific external knowledge and regulatory intelligence (4). Blockchain exploration depended on access to consultants, professional networks and current regulatory developments, while adoption faced legal uncertainty and uneven production standards. Distributed-ledger deployment is therefore conditional on external technical knowledge, integration expertise and continuously updated regulatory interpretation; traceability infrastructure alone cannot resolve institutional uncertainty (c115;c126).
Persistently established dependencies
1. Industrial knowledge-continuity and information-governance architecture (52). Veteran departures removed undocumented maintenance expertise, while experts omitted “obvious” steps or used inconsistent terminology. Digital platforms reduced information silos but introduced privacy, intellectual-property leakage, access-control and data-decay risks. Both traditional elicitation and digital codification therefore require standardized capture procedures, provenance, controlled access, retention policies and expert verification. Without these arrangements, technology relocates rather than eliminates knowledge loss (c101;c105;c108;c117;c121;c122).
2. Production-site hardware, utilities and lifecycle capital (28). AR trials required headsets, batteries and stable tracking; robot learning was limited by onboard computation and low-quality sensors. Pneumatic training required substantial space and compressed-air supply, while laboratories, consultants and adoption programmes were expensive. Deployment remains conditional on suitable premises, utilities, maintainable sensors, replacement equipment and lifecycle funding—not merely initial software acquisition (c103;c106;c107;c119;c126).
3. Protected industrial expertise, staffing and learning time (23). Manufacturing cases reported shortages of skilled workers, specialist BPM and AI expertise, and analysts unfamiliar with expert domains. Knowledge elicitation was time-consuming, while contextual AR information increased learning time. Although generative and semantic tools reduced searching and transcription burdens, organizations still required domain experts and technical translators. Deployment is therefore constrained without protected participation time, interdisciplinary staffing and continuing technical education (c101;c108;c109;c111;c113).
4. Regulatory and safety-compliance architecture (8). Maintenance process models supported compliance, AR equipment could not obstruct shop-floor safety, and viticulture decisions incorporated scientific and legislative requirements. Data-protection rules and evolving blockchain regulation further bounded information use. Technology deployment is therefore conditional on traceable compliance requirements, safety review and jurisdiction-specific data controls (c101;c105;c108;c111;c126).
Together, digitally reconfigured dependencies provide the means to sense, model and rehearse industrial experience, while persistent dependencies supply expertise, physical continuity, governance and legitimacy. Their interaction converts situated know-how into durable organizational knowledge; either layer alone produces technically unreliable or operationally unsupported retention.